source_quote:
When `output_final_state` is `false` (which is the default for vLLM as it consumes the state through in-place cache updates), allocating a new `final_state` tensor via `at::empty_like(initial_state)` is completely unnecessary and introduces significant allocation overhead on every single decode step. Since `inplace_final_state` is `true`, the kernel directly updates `initial_state` in-place, and `final_state` is never written to or read. To avoid this overhead, we can reuse `initial_state` as a placeholder for `final_state` when `output_final_state` is `false`.
> ```suggestion
at::Tensor final_state = output_final_state ? at::empty_like(initial_state) : initial_state;

